# Pools: values that are decided when they are used

A **pool** is a value that has not made up its mind yet. It holds several possible values (equally likely, or with weights), and it picks one **the first time you use it**. After that it stays decided.

```suave
let weather = pool ["rain", "sun", "snow"]
print(inspect(weather))          // pool ["rain", "sun", "snow"] (undecided)

let plans = [weather, weather]   // storing it does not decide it
print("Today: {weather}")        // using it does: now it is, say, "sun"
print(weather, weather)          // sun sun  (it stays decided)
```

Think of a coin in a closed hand, or a quantum state before you measure it.

## Making a pool

```suave
pool [1, 2, 3]                     // equally likely
pool 1..=6                         // a die
pool {"heads": 9, "tails": 1}      // weights: heads is nine times as likely
pool || random.rand()              // a function: a fresh value every time it is decided
pool(["a", "b"], weights: [3, 1])  // the function form
pool [1, 2, 3].where(|n| n > 1)    // ask the pool itself: this is (pool [1, 2, 3]).where(...)
```

`pool` takes just the next value, so `pool [1, 2] + 1` means `(pool [1, 2]) + 1`.

## What counts as "using" a pool

These decide it: arithmetic and comparison (`p + 1`, `p < 3`), `if p`, `match p`, printing it or putting it in a string, calling a method on it that is not a pool method (`p.upper()`), indexing (`p[0]`), looping over it, calling it, passing it to a built-in function (`print`, `len`, `str`, `sqrt` ...), and `p ?? x`.

These do **not** decide it: assigning it, putting it in a list or map, returning it, passing it to your own functions (the function decides when it uses it), and the pool methods below. A pool you share is the same pool everywhere, so when one place decides it, every place sees the same answer.

## Pool methods

They never decide the pool.

| Method | Gives |
| --- | --- |
| `is_decided()` | true once it has been used |
| `peek()` | the decided value, or `none` if it is still open |
| `decide()` | decides it now and gives the value |
| `forget()` | makes it undecided again, so it can be decided afresh |
| `options()` / `size()` | the possible values / how many |
| `chances()` | a map from each value to its probability |
| `odds_of(x)` / `most_likely()` | the probability of x / the likeliest value |
| `where(f)` | a new pool that only keeps options for which `f` is true |
| `map(f)` | a new pool with `f` applied to every option |
| `with_weights(list)` | a new pool with new weights |
| `roll()` / `rolls(n)` | fresh draws that leave the pool undecided |
| `simulate(n)` | how often each value comes up in n fresh draws |
| `entangle(other, ...)` | links pools (below) |
| `copy()` | an independent copy (undecided) |

`inspect(p)` shows a pool without deciding it. `type(p)` is `"pool"`, `is_pool(p)` tests.

## Entangled pools

Pools that are entangled decide together: whichever is used first picks a position, and the others pick the same position.

```suave
let shirt = pool ["red", "green", "blue"]
let shoes = pool ["white", "black", "grey"]
entangle(shirt, shoes)
print("{shirt} with {shoes}")      // red with white, green with black, or blue with grey
```

They need the same number of options. `forget()` on one forgets them all.

## Repeatable

Pools use the shared random generator, so `random.seed(42)` makes a whole program (pools, `std.random` and quantum measurements) give the same answers every run.

## std.pool

Ready-made pools: `pool.die(6)`, `pool.dice("2d6+1")`, `pool.coin()`, `pool.weighted({...})`, `pool.uniform(a, b)`, `pool.normal(mu, sigma)`, `pool.exponential(rate)`, `pool.poisson(l)`, `pool.shuffle(list)` (a pool of orderings), `pool.deal(list, k)` (a hand of cards), `pool.decide_all(list)`. Import it as `use std.pool as pools` if you also want to write `pool [...]` in the same file.

## Pools and quantum

A quantum register can give you a pool that measures only when you look: `reg.measure_later(0)` (the measurement really happens, and collapses the state, when the pool is first used) and `reg.pool()` (a weighted pool over all the possible outcomes that does not touch the register). See [QUANTUM.md](#/suave-quantum).
